Write a self-contained prompt that asks an AI coding agent to recreate a Python
module implementing a class for searching English subtitles on OpenSubtitles.
Create a single-file Python 3 script (intended to run under Termux on Android,
so include the Termux-style shebang line
`#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`) that defines a
`SubtitleDownloader` class for searching subtitles on the OpenSubtitles REST
API.
Requirements: **Purpose:** The script provides a downloader helper that searches
OpenSubtitles for English-language subtitles matching a free-text query, such as
a movie or show name or a release filename. **Imports and dependencies:** - Use
`from __future__ import annotations` at the top. - Use the standard library
modules `re` and `sys`, and `quote` from `urllib.parse`. - Use the third-party
`requests` library for HTTP. **Class `SubtitleDownloader`:** - The constructor
creates a `requests.Session` stored on `self.session`, and sets its headers to
include a browser-like User-Agent string: `Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64;
x64) AppleWebKit/537.36`. - A method `search_opensubtitles(self, query)`
performs the search: - Normalize the query by replacing underscores and periods
with spaces. - Send a GET request to
`https://rest.opensubtitles.com/api/v1/subtitles` with the query parameters
`query` (the normalized string), `languages` set to `en`, and `type` set to
`all`.
Use the session for the request. - If the HTTP status is 200, parse the JSON
body.
If the `data` key is present and non-empty, iterate through its items.
Each item has an `attributes` object (default to an empty dict if missing).
Return the first item whose `attributes.language` equals `en`, as a dictionary
with keys: - `id`: the item's `id` (required; use direct key access) - `title`:
the attribute `title`, defaulting to the original (un-normalized) query string -
`url`: the attribute `url` (may be None) - `files`: the attribute `files`,
defaulting to an empty list - If no matching English item is found, or the
status is not 200, or `data` is empty, return `None`. - Wrap the whole operation
in a try/except that catches any `Exception`, prints `Error searching
OpenSubtitles: <error>` to standard output, and returns `None`. **Behavior notes
and edge cases:** - No timeout, retry, or authentication headers are specified;
the API key is not used in this version. - The method does not raise exceptions
to callers; all failures result in `None`. - The `re`, `sys`, and `quote`
imports are present in the original module; include them even though this
portion does not need to use them, so the module matches the original structure.
- The script has no `main` block or CLI in this portion; it only defines the
class so it can be imported or extended. **Output expectations:** Produce only
the complete Python file content, with the shebang, imports, and class as
described, following standard PEP 8 style and a 4-space indent. ---
